# Chapter 5: Results and Analysis

## 5.1 Performance Evaluation

### 5.1.1 Evaluation Criteria and Testing Methods

The performance of the hybrid crowd density estimation system is quantified using a comprehensive set of metrics that address accuracy, efficiency, and calibration. These metrics are consistent with those employed in established crowd counting literature [Li et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2020].

**Mean Absolute Error (MAE):** The primary accuracy metric for crowd counting tasks. Computed as:

MAE = (1/N) · Σᵢ₌₁ᴺ |Ĉᵢ − Cᵢ|

where Ĉᵢ is the predicted count and Cᵢ is the ground-truth count for image i. MAE provides an easily interpretable measure of average absolute counting error across the evaluation set.

**Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE):** Penalises large individual prediction errors more heavily than MAE:

RMSE = √[ (1/N) · Σᵢ₌₁ᴺ (Ĉᵢ − Cᵢ)² ]

RMSE is a useful indicator of error variance and the influence of outlier predictions.

**95% Bootstrap Confidence Intervals for MAE:** Constructed using 2,000-iteration bootstrap resampling on the 500-image validation set. Each iteration re-samples 500 images with replacement and computes the MAE on the resampled set. The 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the resulting distribution define the confidence interval. These provide statistically grounded uncertainty estimates for reported MAE values.

**Router Accuracy:** The fraction of NWPU-Crowd validation images for which the routing classifier assigns the correct binary routing label (sparse or dense, as defined by ground-truth count relative to T = 100).

**Expected Calibration Error (ECE):** Measures the alignment between predicted routing confidence and empirical accuracy using a 10-bin uniform-width calibration scheme. A lower ECE indicates that the router's confidence scores are well-calibrated.

**Inference Latency:** Average per-image inference time in milliseconds, measured on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050 (8 GB GDDR6) after 30 GPU warmup passes and averaged over 30 subsequent evaluation passes. CPU latency is additionally measured as a reference for edge CPU deployment.

**Testing Protocol:** All primary evaluation is conducted on the 500-image NWPU-Crowd validation set, which provides publicly available ground-truth annotations. Cross-dataset generalisation is evaluated on the ShanghaiTech Part A (182 test images) and Part B (316 test images) test sets, with no fine-tuning or exposure of any system component to these datasets during training.

---

### 5.1.2 Primary Evaluation Results — NWPU-Crowd Validation Set

Table 5.1 presents the complete comparative evaluation of all system configurations on the 500-image NWPU-Crowd validation set.

**[Table 5.1 — Complete System Comparison — NWPU-Crowd Validation Set (500 images)]**

| Model Configuration | MAE ↓ | RMSE ↓ | 95% CI (MAE) | GPU Latency (ms) | Params |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| LCDNet Only | 348.0 | 1,033.9 | [269.5, 443.1] | 40.7 | 0.92M |
| MobileCount (baseline, no KD) | 213.2 | 803.6 | [155.4, 292.6] | 30.5 | 0.88M |
| MobileCount (distilled) | 198.7 | 778.5 | [143.8, 275.1] | 30.5 | 0.88M |
| Hybrid-Hard (baseline MC, p*=0.50) | 183.0 | 787.7 | [126.2, 260.3] | 53.4 | 4.35M |
| Hybrid-Soft (fusion) | 182.6 | 763.8 | [127.9, 259.0] | 56.0 | 4.35M |
| Hybrid-Hard (distilled MC, p*=0.50) | 182.4 | 764.0 | [127.8, 258.6] | 55.0 | 4.35M |
| **Hybrid-Hard (distilled MC, p*=0.85)** | **180.9** | **763.6** | — | **~55** | **4.35M** |
| Oracle Router (upper bound) | 166.6 | 751.1 | [112.9, 241.5] | — | — |

The best-performing configuration — the hard-routing hybrid with the knowledge-distilled dense specialist at the calibrated threshold p* = 0.85 — achieves a system MAE of 180.9, outperforming both deployed specialists operating independently (348.0 for LCDNet and 198.7 for distilled MobileCount). The gap between the hybrid and the oracle upper bound is 14.3 MAE points, representing the quantified cost of the router's ~11% misclassification rate on the validation set.

