## 5.2 Analysis of Design Solutions

### 5.2.1 Ablation Study

A comprehensive ablation study was conducted to isolate and quantify the contribution of each individual design element to the final system performance. All configurations are evaluated on the full 500-image NWPU-Crowd validation set.

**[Table 5.4 — Ablation Study Results]**

| Configuration | Description | MAE | RMSE | ΔMAE vs. Full System |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Full System** | Distilled MC + Router p*=0.85 | **180.9** | **763.6** | — |
| A1: No Distillation | Baseline MC + Router p*=0.50 | 183.0 | 787.7 | +2.1 |
| A2: No Routing (dense) | Distilled MC for all inputs | 198.7 | 778.5 | +17.8 |
| A3: No Routing (sparse) | LCDNet for all inputs | 348.0 | 1,034.4 | +167.1 |
| A4: No Threshold Sweep | Distilled MC + Router p*=0.50 | 182.4 | 764.0 | +1.5 |
| A5: Soft Fusion | Distilled MC + LCDNet blended | 182.6 | 763.8 | +1.7 |

**Finding 1 — Routing is the Dominant Contributor:** Removing the router entirely (A2) raises system MAE by 17.8 points. This is the single largest performance degradation in the ablation and confirms that adaptive model selection is the principal mechanism driving system accuracy.

**Finding 2 — Knowledge Distillation is the Second Contributor:** Replacing the distilled MobileCount with the baseline MobileCount (A1) raises MAE by 2.1 points at zero additional deployment cost — the distilled model has identical parameters, GFLOPs, and latency. Distillation is therefore a highly cost-effective accuracy improvement mechanism.

**Finding 3 — Threshold Optimisation Contributes Measurably:** Using the default argmax threshold (p* = 0.50) rather than the calibrated threshold (p* = 0.85) raises MAE by 1.5 points (A4). This improvement is obtained post-training with no model modifications, representing a free performance gain.

**Finding 4 — Soft Fusion Offers No Advantage:** Running both specialists and blending their outputs (A5) consistently produces higher MAE than hard routing with the optimal threshold (182.6 vs. 180.9), while also increasing latency marginally. Soft fusion is therefore excluded from the final system configuration.

