### 5.2.2 Evaluation Criteria — Accuracy, Efficiency, Feasibility

**Accuracy:** The proposed hybrid system achieves a system MAE of 180.9 on the 500-image NWPU-Crowd validation set with a 95% bootstrap confidence interval in the range [127.8, 258.6]. The system outperforms both deployed specialists independently. The accuracy gap relative to the oracle upper bound (166.6) is 14.3 MAE — the cost of imperfect routing.

**Efficiency:** The full deployed system requires 4.354M parameters across its three components (Router 2.552M + LCDNet 0.917M + MobileCount 0.884M). This compares favourably with heavy single models such as CSRNet (16.26M), representing a 73.3% reduction in deployed parameters while maintaining a competitive accuracy profile. Average end-to-end latency of approximately 55 ms (≈18 FPS) is compatible with near-real-time surveillance applications on modern edge hardware.

**Feasibility:** The system was trained within approximately 24 hours on an NVIDIA RTX 3050 (8 GB) consumer-grade GPU, demonstrating that the computational requirements for training are within reach of academic and industrial research teams without access to high-performance computing clusters.

**Strengths:**
1. Modular architecture: any component can be replaced without retraining the others.
2. Privacy-preserving: all inference is on-device; no raw video transmission.
3. Adaptive: routing dynamically adjusts resource allocation to scene complexity.
4. Knowledge distillation provides measurable accuracy improvement at zero deployment cost.

**Weaknesses:**
1. Routing errors near the density boundary (counts 80–120) cause non-negligible misrouting.
2. Both specialists saturate on extreme-density scenes (count > 3,000), producing large undercount errors.
3. The router adds ~5 ms latency overhead per image even when dense-specialist accuracy is not needed.

