## 5.4 Statistical Analysis

### 5.4.1 Bootstrap Confidence Interval Analysis

The 95% bootstrap confidence intervals for MAE are reported in Table 5.1. A key observation is that the confidence intervals for the full hybrid system and MobileCount standalone overlap significantly:

- Hybrid-Hard (distilled, p*=0.85): CI not reported (point estimate 180.9)
- Hybrid-Hard (distilled, p*=0.50): [127.8, 258.6]
- MobileCount (distilled): [143.8, 275.1]

The overlapping intervals indicate that the 17.8-point MAE improvement of the hybrid over standalone MobileCount, while consistent, does not reach conventional statistical significance at α = 0.05 on the 500-image validation set alone. This is attributable to the high variance in the error distribution introduced by a small number of extreme-density failure cases. The practical significance of the routing gain is more clearly demonstrated by stratified analysis (Table 5.2), where the sparse stratum shows a 60.2% MAE reduction (84.5 → 33.6) — a robust and reproducible effect.

### 5.4.2 Error Distribution and Variance Analysis

**[Table 5.5 — Error Distribution Statistical Summary — Hybrid-Hard (Distilled, p*=0.85)]**

| Statistic | Value |
|---|---|
| MAE | 180.9 |
| RMSE | 763.6 |
| RMSE/MAE Ratio | 4.22 |
| Median Absolute Error | ~34 |
| Fraction of images with |error| < 50 | ~52% |
| Fraction of images with |error| < 100 | ~62% |
| Contribution of top-5 worst cases to MAE | ~25 MAE points |

The RMSE/MAE ratio of 4.22 is high relative to the ratio expected for a Gaussian error distribution (approximately 1.25), indicating the presence of heavy-tailed errors. Five extreme-density images (GT count > 5,000) dominate the squared error distribution. Image 3234 alone (GT = 12,924; predicted = 10 due to router catastrophic misroute) contributes approximately 25 MAE points to the 500-image mean. Excluding this single image reduces hybrid MAE from 183.0 to approximately 158, demonstrating the disproportionate influence of rare routing failures on aggregate metrics.

### 5.4.3 Router Calibration Analysis

Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.095 indicates moderate miscalibration of the routing classifier (Table 5.6). Disaggregated analysis reveals that the miscalibration is concentrated in the 65–95% confidence range. In the highest-confidence bin ([0.95, 1.0]), 442 of the 500 validation images fall with an empirical accuracy of 92.8%, confirming that high-confidence routing decisions are reliable. The 11.2% misrouted images (56 of 500) are concentrated in the intermediate confidence region, where scene density is genuinely near the routing boundary.

**[Table 5.6 — Router Calibration by Confidence Bin (Selected Bins)]**

| Confidence Range | Images in Bin | Empirical Accuracy | Calibration Error |
|---|---|---|---|
| [0.50, 0.65) | 12 | 75.0% | Moderate |
| [0.65, 0.80) | 22 | 72.7% | High |
| [0.80, 0.95) | 24 | 83.3% | Moderate |
| [0.95, 1.00] | 442 | 92.8% | Low |
| **Overall ECE** | 500 | 88.8% | **0.095** |

