## 5.5 Comparisons and Relationships

### 5.5.1 Cross-Dataset Generalisation

All models were trained exclusively on NWPU-Crowd with no exposure to ShanghaiTech during training, validation, or hyperparameter selection. Cross-dataset generalisation results are presented in Tables 5.7 and 5.8.

**[Table 5.7 — Cross-Dataset Evaluation: ShanghaiTech Part B (316 test images, predominantly sparse)]**

| Model | MAE ↓ | RMSE ↓ |
|---|---|---|
| LCDNet Only | 74.1 | 111.1 |
| MobileCount (distilled) | 41.7 | 53.5 |
| **Hybrid-Hard** | **35.2** | **50.8** |
| Oracle Router | 29.3 | 45.1 |

**[Table 5.8 — Cross-Dataset Evaluation: ShanghaiTech Part A (182 test images, predominantly dense)]**

| Model | MAE ↓ | RMSE ↓ |
|---|---|---|
| LCDNet Only | 340.2 | 485.8 |
| MobileCount (distilled) | 132.3 | 213.1 |
| **Hybrid-Hard** | **133.1** | **212.8** |
| Oracle Router | 125.7 | 206.5 |

On ShanghaiTech Part B — a predominantly sparse dataset — the hybrid achieves MAE 35.2, outperforming both standalone specialists (41.7 and 74.1) without any fine-tuning, demonstrating that the routing mechanism successfully transfers to an unseen dataset. The improvement over standalone MobileCount (6.5 MAE points) is attributable to the router correctly identifying sparse images and dispatching them to LCDNet.

On ShanghaiTech Part A — a predominantly dense dataset — the hybrid performs comparably to MobileCount standalone (133.1 vs. 132.3). This is expected: the router correctly identifies the majority of dense images and dispatches them to MobileCount, so the routing mechanism adds no additional noise on a dataset dominated by dense scenes. The close agreement between the hybrid and the oracle (133.1 vs. 125.7) confirms robust cross-domain routing behaviour.

