### 5.5.3 Failure Case Analysis

**[Table 5.11 — Representative Failure Cases — NWPU-Crowd Validation Set]**

| Image ID | GT Count | LCDNet Pred. | MC Pred. | Hybrid Pred. | Root Cause |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 3234 | 12,924 | 10 | 158 | **10** | Router: 99% confident sparse — catastrophic miss |
| 3408 | 9,728 | 78 | 2,428 | 2,428 | Both specialists saturate at extreme density |
| 3353 | 7,122 | 28 | 2,077 | 2,077 | Density beyond lightweight capacity |
| 3587 | 6,799 | 60 | 2,301 | 2,301 | Same |
| 3146 | 5,951 | 35 | 2,265 | 2,265 | Same |

Two qualitatively distinct failure types are observed. The first — exemplified by image 3234 — is a high-confidence routing catastrophe: the router assigns 99% sparse probability to an image whose ground-truth count is 12,924. This single image accounts for approximately 25 MAE points of the 500-image aggregate. The second failure type — exemplified by images 3408, 3353, 3587, and 3146 — is a capacity saturation failure: both specialists are correctly routed to MobileCount, but neither lightweight model can accurately represent the extreme density, producing large systematic undercount errors. Routing is not the cause of these failures; even the oracle router cannot recover them.

